Title: Cadeville
Order: 5
Production Code: 1x05

FADE IN:

INT. STOWE UNIVERSITY - PRESIDENT'S OFFICE - DAY

The Legislature of Louisiana has passed a law to prevent white people from teaching in schools conducted in the interest of Negroes. The white minister from the North who presided over the Negro college at Cadeville has been forced to resign. The colored people write to the president of Stowe University asking him to recommend a man competent to take charge. Dr. Lovejoy decides that Belton Piedmont is an ideal man for the place. Belton is duly elected.

INT. TRAIN - FIRST-CLASS COACH - DAY

SUPER: "LOUISIANA."

At that time the law providing separate coaches had not been enacted by any of the Southern States, but in some of them the whites had an unwritten but inexorable law that no Negro should ride in a first-class coach. Louisiana was one. Belton does not know this.

He sits quietly reading a newspaper. Now and then he looks out at the pine forest near the track. The railway bed is elevated two or three feet, and beside it runs a trench, full of muddy water after days of rain.

The coach fills. A white lady enters, pauses to look for a seat, and finds one. Her slight difficulty has been noted by the white passengers. Belton sees a group of white men in eager conversation, glancing in his direction. He pays no attention.

Before he knows it, he is surrounded by angry men. He stands up in surprise.

SPOKESMAN
Get out of this coach. We don't allow niggers in first-class coaches. Get out at once.

BELTON
Show me your authority to order me out, sir.

SPOKESMAN
We are our own authority, as you will soon find out if you don't get out of here.

BELTON
I propose to stay right in this coach as long----

Rough fingers clutch his throat. They overpower him, drag him into the aisle, and bear him, some at his head and others at his feet, to the door. On the platform, instead of carrying him across to the second-class coach, they toss him off the speeding train.

EXT. LOUISIANA RAILWAY EMBANKMENT - CONTINUOUS

His body and feet fall into the water of the muddy ditch; his head buries itself in the soft clay bed. The train speeds on.

He extricates himself. Covered from head to foot with red clay, the president-elect of Cadeville College walks to the next station, two miles away, where he finds his satchel, left by the conductor. In the afternoon he rides second-class to Monroe, Louisiana.

EXT. MONROE, LOUISIANA - NIGHT

A city of some twenty thousand inhabitants. The afternoon train does not connect with the Cadeville train. He must remain overnight.

INT. MONROE - RESTAURANT - NIGHT

Hungry, he asks the white man behind the counter for a meal. The man steps into an adjoining room to fill the order. Belton sits on a high stool at the counter. The man returns with food in a paper bag.

CLERK
Get up from there, you nigger. It would cost me a hundred dollars for you to be seen sitting there.

BELTON
Do you mean to say that I must stand up here and eat?

CLERK
No, I don't mean any such thing. You must go out of here to eat.

BELTON
Then I shall politely leave your food on your hands if I cannot be allowed to eat in here.

CLERK
I guess you won't. I have cut this ham off for you and you have got to take it.

Belton starts for the door. The man seizes a whistle. Two or three policemen come running, followed by a crowd.

CLERK (CONT'D)
This high-toned nigger bought a meal of me and because I would not let him sit down and eat like white people he refused to pay me.

POLICEMAN
Pay that man what you owe him.

BELTON
I owe him nothing. He refuses to accommodate me, and I therefore owe him nothing.

POLICEMAN
Come along with me, sir. Consider yourself under arrest.

INT. MONROE - MAYOR'S COURT - MORNING

He has been locked up in jail for the night. The mayor, whom the officer has evidently posted, fines him five dollars for vagrancy and orders him to leave town within five hours. He pays and boards the train for Cadeville.

EXT. CADEVILLE - RAILWAY PLATFORM - DAY

A group of white men stand on the platform. One is a thin, scrawny man with a long, very white beard, stooping slightly forward, bending as if to see you better: the village doctor. As Belton steps down, the doctor looks at him keenly.

Belton is a fine specimen of physical manhood; his limbs well formed, proportioned and seemingly strong as oak.

The doctor goes up to the postmaster, a short man with a large head, president of the band of "Nigger Rulers" of that section.

DR. ZACKLAND
I'll be durned if that ain't the finest lookin' darkey I ever put my eye on. If I could get his body to dissect, I'd give one of the finest kegs of whiskey in my cellar.

THE CADEVILLE POSTMASTER
Zakeland, you are right. He is a fine looking chap, and he looks a little tony. If we "nigger rulers" are ever called in to attend to him we will not burn him nor shoot him to pieces. We will kill him kinder decent and let you have him to dissect. I shall not fail to call for that whiskey to treat the boys.

They part. Belton does not hear this murderous conversation. He is joyfully received by the colored people of Cadeville, to whom he relates his experiences. They look at him as a superior being bearing a charmed life. It does not occur to them to be surprised at his treatment for what he had done; their wonder is that he got off so easily.

INT. CADEVILLE COLLEGE - DAY

Belton takes charge of the school. He resolves to add an industrial department. He travels over the state and secures the funds. He sends to New Orleans for a colored architect and contractor, who draw the plans and take the contract.

EXT. CADEVILLE COLLEGE - CONSTRUCTION SITE - DAY

Colored men erect the building. The white brick-masons of Monroe organize a mob, come to Cadeville and order the men to quit work. They take charge of the work themselves, letting the colored brick-masons act as hod carriers. They employ a white man to supervise. The colored people know it means death to resist. They pay the men as though nothing unusual had happened.

Belton has learned to observe and wait. These outrages sink like molten lead into his heart.

INT. CADEVILLE COLLEGE - CHAPEL - MORNING

The time for the presidential election draws near. Belton arises in the chapel to lecture the young men on their duty to vote.

INT. CADEVILLE - A HOUSE - DAY

A village girl tells her father. The old man is shaving. He has just shaved off one side of his beard.

EXT. CADEVILLE - ROAD - DAY

He does not stop to pull the towel from around his neck nor to put down his razor. He rushes to the house where Belton boards.

INT. BELTON'S ROOM - CADEVILLE - DAY

He bursts in. Belton throws up his hands in alarm at the man with razor in hand, towel round his neck, beard half off and half on. The man sits to catch his breath.

OLD MAN
Mr. Piedmont, I learn that you are advising our young men to vote. I am sure you don't know in what danger you stand. I have come to give you the political history of this section of Louisiana. The colored people of this region far outnumber the white people, and years ago had absolute control of everything. The whites of course did not tamely submit, but armed themselves to overthrow us. We armed ourselves, and every night patrolled this road all night long looking for the whites to come and attack us. My oldest brother is a very cowardly and sycophantic man. The white people made a spy and traitor out of him. When the people found out that there was treachery in our ranks it demoralized them, and our organization went to pieces.

OLD MAN (CONT'D)
We had not the authority nor disposition to kill a traitor, and consequently we had no effective remedy against a betrayal. When the news of our demoralized condition reached the whites it gave them fresh courage, and they have dominated us ever since. They carry on the elections. We stay in our fields all day long on election day and scarcely know what is going on. Not long since a white man came through here and distributed republican ballots. The white people captured him and cut his body into four pieces and threw it in the Ouachita River. Since then you can't get any man to venture here to distribute ballots.

OLD MAN (CONT'D)
Just before the last presidential campaign, two brothers, Samuel and John Bowser, colored, happened to go down to New Orleans. Things are not so bad down there. These two brothers each secured a republican party ballot, and on election day somewhat boastfully cast them into the ballot box. There is a society here known as "Nigger Rulers." The postmaster of this place is president of the society, and the teacher of the white public school is the captain of the army thereof.

EXT. BOWSER HOUSE - NIGHT (OLD MAN V.O.)

They sent word to the Bowser brothers that they would soon be there to whip them. The brothers cut a hole in the front side of the house, through which to poke a gun. Night comes and, true to their word, the "Nigger Rulers" come. Samuel Bowser fires when they are near the house and one man falls dead. The rest flee to the woods. They return cautiously, bear away their dead comrade, make no further attack that night.

EXT. THE WOODS - THREE DAYS (OLD MAN V.O.)

The brothers hide in the woods. The whites hem in the entire settlement on all sides. On the third night a great rain comes up and the whites seek shelter. John escapes into Arkansas. Samuel, poor fool, goes only forty miles, remaining in Louisiana. The mob forces one of the colored men who escorted him on horseback to tell the road he took. They trace and find him, tie him on a horse, bring him back, keep him in the woods three days, torturing him. On the third day a gun's loud report is heard. None knows where he lies buried.

INT. BELTON'S ROOM - CADEVILLE - DAY

OLD MAN
You can judge from this why we neglect voting.

This speech winds up Belton's political career in Cadeville. He thanks the man for the information, assuring him it will be of great value in knowing how to shape his course.

INT. CADEVILLE COLLEGE - COMMENCEMENT - DAY (A FEW YEARS LATER)

Belton has had a number of young men and women graduate. He has invited the pastor of a leading white church of Monroe to deliver an oration. The preacher is most favorably impressed with Belton's students and esteems him a man of great intellectual power, and invites him to call at his church and house if he ever comes to Monroe.

INT. MONROE - WHITE CHURCH - SUNDAY MORNING

One Sabbath Belton happens to be in Monroe. He goes to the church, enters, and takes a seat near the middle. During the opening exercises a young white lady beside him has trouble finding the hymn. Belton, who remembers the number given out, kindly takes the book to find it.

In an instant the whole church is in an uproar. A crowd of men gather round Belton and lead him out of doors. A few leaders go off to one side and hold a short consultation. They decide that as it is Sunday they will not lynch him. They order him released. This evidently does not please the majority, but he is allowed to go.

EXT. MONROE - THE PREACHER'S HOUSE - AFTERNOON

Belton calls at the residence of the minister to offer an explanation. The minister opens the door, sees who it is, and slams it in his face.

INT. MONROE - CHURCH - SUNDAY

Dr. Zackland, who always spends Sundays at Monroe, has witnessed the entire scene. He hurries out of church, sees Belton turned loose, hurries to the station and boards the train for Cadeville, leaving his hymn book and Bible on the seat. His face seems lighted with joy.

DR. ZACKLAND
I've got him at last. Careful as he has been I've got him.

INT. CADEVILLE - POSTMASTER'S HOUSE - SUNDAY

He runs to the postmaster's house, out of breath. He sits, fans himself with his hat.

DR. ZACKLAND
Well, we will have to fix that nigger, Piedmont. He is getting too high.

THE CADEVILLE POSTMASTER
What's that he has been doing now? I have looked upon him as being an uncommonly good nigger. I have kept a good eye on him but haven't even had to hint at him.

DR. ZACKLAND
Well, he has shown his true nature at last. He had the gall to enter a white church in Monroe this morning and actually took a seat down stairs with the white folks; he did not even look at the gallery where he belonged.

THE CADEVILLE POSTMASTER
Is that so?

DR. ZACKLAND
I should say he did, and that's not all. A white girl who sat by him and could not read very well, failed to find the hymn at once. That nigger actually had the impudence to take her book and find the place for her.

THE CADEVILLE POSTMASTER
The infernal scoundrel. By golly, he shall hang.

DR. ZACKLAND
Naturally the congregation was infuriated and soon hustled the impudent scoundrel out. If services had not been going on, and if it had not been Sunday, there is no telling what would have happened. I came here to tell you, as he is our "Nigger" living here at Cadeville, and the "Nigger Rulers" of Cadeville will be disrespected if they let such presumptuous niggers go about to disturb religious services.

THE CADEVILLE POSTMASTER
You are right about that, and we must soon put him out of the way. To-night will be his last night on earth.

DR. ZACKLAND
Do you remember our bargain that we made about that nigger when he came about here?

THE CADEVILLE POSTMASTER
No.

DR. ZACKLAND
Well, I do. I have been all along itching for a chance to carry it out. You were to give me the nigger's body for dissecting purposes, in return for which I was to give you a keg of my best whiskey.

THE CADEVILLE POSTMASTER
(laughing)
Ha, ha, ha. I do remember it now.

DR. ZACKLAND
Well, I'll certainly stick up to my part of the program if you will stick to yours. I have a suggestion to make about the taking off of the nigger. Don't have any burning or riddling with bullets. Just hang him and fire one shot in the back of his head. I want him whole in the interest of society. That whiskey will be the finest that you will ever have and I want a good bargain for it.

THE CADEVILLE POSTMASTER
I'll follow your instructions to the letter. I'll just tell the boys that he, being a kind of decent nigger, we will give him a decent hanging. Meantime, Doctor, I must get out. To-day is Sunday and we must do our work to-morrow night. I must get a meeting of the boys to-night.

EXT. CADEVILLE - NIGHT (MONDAY)

About nine o'clock a mob of the "Nigger Rulers" comes and takes Belton out into the neighboring woods. He is given five minutes to pray, at the end of which he is to be hanged. He seems to have foreseen the coming of the mob, but feels somehow that God is at work to deliver him. He makes no resistance, having unshaken faith in God.

EXT. THE WOODS - NIGHT

The rope is adjusted round his neck and thrown over the limb of a tree. Belton swings up. The postmaster slips forward and fires his pistol at the base of his skull. Blood comes oozing forth. He orders the men to retire, as he does not care for them to remain to shoot holes in the body, as is their custom.

When they have gone, three men sent by Dr. Zackland steal out of hiding and cut Belton down. He has been hanging only seven minutes; the bullet has not entered the skull but has simply ploughed its way under the skin. He is unconscious and to all appearances dead.

INT. DR. ZACKLAND'S HOUSE - REAR ROOM - NIGHT

The three men bear him to the doctor's residence and enter a rear door. They lay him on a dissecting table in the room where the doctor performs all surgical operations.

Dr. Zackland looks down on Belton with a happy smile. To have such a robust, well-formed, handsome nigger to dissect and examine he regards as one of the greatest boons of his medical career.

The three men start to retire.

DR. ZACKLAND
Wait, let us see if he is dead.

Belton has returned to consciousness but keeps his eyes closed. Dr. Zackland cuts off the hair near the wound and begins cutting the skin, tracing the bullet. Belton does not wince.

DR. ZACKLAND (CONT'D)
The nigger is dead or else he would show some sign of life. But I will try pricking his palm.

He does. The pain is exceedingly excruciating. Belton shows no sign of feeling.

DR. ZACKLAND (CONT'D)
You may go now. He is certainly dead.

The men leave. Dr. Zackland takes out his watch.

DR. ZACKLAND (CONT'D)
It is now 10 o'clock. Those doctors from Monroe will be here by twelve. I can have everything exactly ready by that time.

A bright ray of hope in Belton's bosom: two hours more of life, two hours in which to plan an escape. The doctor stirs about the room. He takes a long, sharp knife, gazes at its edge, and lays it on the table near Belton's feet. He goes out for a pail of water, leaving the door ajar. He goes to his cabinet for more instruments; his back is turned.

Belton's eyes, only slightly open, have followed every movement. He raises himself, seizes the knife, and at a bound is at the doctor's side. The doctor turns, in dread alarm at the sight of the dead man returned to life, too terrified to act or scream. Belton plunges the knife through his throat.

Seizing the dying man he lays him on the dissecting board and covers him with a sheet. At the writing desk he scrawls a note and pins it to the sheet where it would attract attention at once if one began to uncover the corpse.

INSERT - THE NOTE

"DOCTORS: I have stepped out for a short while. Don't touch the nigger until I come. Zackland."

He creeps out, leaps the back fence, goes to his boarding place, changes his clothes, and disappears in the woods.

INT. BATON ROUGE - GOVERNOR'S OFFICE - DAY

He makes his way to Baton Rouge and seeks a conference with the Governor. The Governor orders him under arrest and tells him that the best and only thing he can do is send him back to Cadeville under military escort to be tried for murder.

INT. CADEVILLE COURTHOUSE - DAY

The community is aroused over the death of Dr. Zackland at the hands of a negro. The sending of the military further incenses them. At the trial all evidence respecting the mob is excluded as irrelevant. Robbery is the motive assigned. The whole family with which Belton lives is arraigned as accomplices, because his bloody clothes were found in his room in their house.

The jury are allowed to walk about and mingle freely with the people and be influenced by the bitter public sentiment against Belton. Men who were in the mob are on the jury. The postmaster is foreman. Without leaving their seats they return a verdict of guilty in each case, and all are sentenced to be hanged.

INT. NEW ORLEANS JAIL - NIGHT

The prisoners are taken to the New Orleans jail for safe keeping. A newspaper reporter of a liberal New Orleans paper calls on them. He is impressed with Belton's personality and promises to publish any statement Belton will write. Belton gives a thorough, detailed account of every happening. The story is telegraphed broadcast and arouses sympathetic interest everywhere.

INT. NORFOLK - BERNARD'S OFFICE - DAY

Bernard Belgrave reads an account of it and hastens to his friend's side in New Orleans. He telegraphs a certain influential democratic senator, who comes to New Orleans.

INT. NEW ORLEANS JAIL - DAY

Bernard and Belton. Bernard has come to his friend's side.

INT. UNITED STATES SUPREME COURT - DAY

Influence is brought to bear; though all precedent is violated, the case is manoeuvred to the Supreme Court of the United States. Bernard makes the speech of his life; competent judges say the like of it has not been heard since the days of Daniel Webster. As he pleads for his friend and the others accused, the judges of the Supreme Court weep scalding tears. Bernard tells of Belton's noble life, his unassuming ways, his pure Christianity.

The decision of the lower court is reversed, a change of venue granted, a new trial held, and an acquittal secured.

CUT TO:
